PRIORITY GAPS TO CLOSE

Gap 1: LinkedIn is not a landing page for SD3, it is a job description for your current role
Your headline and About section are written backward. They tell people what you do today.
Recruiters searching for an SD3 need to land on a profile that proves you are already thinking
and operating at that level. The Featured section has your resume uploaded, which signals
passivity. There is zero LinkedIn activity, which means the algorithm is not surfacing you.
Gap 2: No impact document, so every application starts from scratch
You are rewriting bullets from memory each time. That is why some bullets lack metrics and
why the same work looks different across applications. An impact document fixes this
permanently. You said yourself that most of your work is internal, so you do not need public
proof. You need a private structured record that you can pull from when writing for a specific job
description.
Gap 3: Outreach reads like a job seeker, not a problem solver
You are messaging recruiters and mentioning the role you want, then attaching context about
your background. That is the format almost everyone uses, which means it gets the response
rate almost everyone gets: close to zero. The shift is from 'here is who I am' to 'here is what I
know about the problem you are trying to solve and here is one example of me solving it.' That
one change transforms how you are perceived before a call even happens.
Gap 4: Application volume with low focus is tiring and low-yield
You described applying broadly through LinkedIn, getting calls mostly from startups. The
pattern is consistent with what happens when a strong candidate competes on quantity rather
than fit. Narrowing to 5 to 10 target companies at a time and building a pipeline within each one
outperforms 200 applications reliably.
Gap 5: The senior-room question at Agoda
When the hiring committee elevated your loop to SD3/Staff and the director asked how you
manage resources and push for headcount, the expected answer is a story: a specific time you
influenced without authority, built a case for investment, or navigated ambiguity at the team
level. This is behavioral territory and it needs prepared stories ready to go, not improvised
answers.

