LINKEDIN OVERHAUL: WHAT TO CHANGE AND WHY
Headline: Rewrite it for the role you are targeting, not the role you are in
Current headline describes your employer and tech stack. It is not wrong, but it is invisible to
recruiters searching for SD3 talent. Replace it with something that names the level you are
going for and the domain you own.
Option A: Senior Software Engineer | Distributed Systems & Data Platform | Scaling
infrastructure at Uber (100k+ RPM, 2M+ assets)
Option B: SD3-Level Backend Engineer | Data Platform, AI Tooling, Real-Time Systems | Open
to senior roles in India and globally
Option C: Software Engineer II at Uber | Distributed Systems, AI-Native Infrastructure |
Targeting Staff/SD3 at high-scale product companies

About Section: Make it a narrative, not a spec sheet
Your current About is a formal third-person summary of technologies. Rewrite it in first person.
Open with the type of problem you are drawn to, name two or three concrete things you have
built and their impact, and close with what you are looking for and how to reach you. Recruiters
read this the way a hiring manager reads a cover letter. Make it earn its place.
Featured Section: Remove the resume, add impact artifacts
Uploading your resume to Featured communicates that you are looking for a job. That is not the
signal you want. Instead, use this space to show work. Two or three posts or articles that walk
through a problem you solved, how you approached it, and what the result was. Even a short
write-up on GitHub or Medium about your LangGraph multi-agent system at Uber would make
a strong Featured item. Screenshots of dashboards with context are fine. The point is to make
someone curious enough to reach out.
Skills Section: Move top skills to match SD3 keyword expectations
