Activity: Post at minimum twice a month, comment on three posts a week
You have no visible activity on LinkedIn. LinkedIn's algorithm is straightforward: active profiles
surface in search results, inactive profiles do not. You do not need to write long articles. Short
posts about something interesting you encountered at work, a technical tradeoff you thought
about, or a lesson from building the multi-agent system all qualify. Writing about the Data
Co-Pilot architecture with one concrete takeaway would land very well in the data engineering
and AI community.
Recommendations: Solicit two to three from former managers or senior peers
A specific recommendation from a manager or tech lead at Zomato describing the WebSocket
rewrite or the Merchant Outlet work would carry real weight. Generic praise does not move the
needle. Impact-specific praise does. Ask your recommenders to name the project, the scale,
and one outcome they observed directly.

RESUME: WHAT TO FIX NOW
Your resume has good raw material and it is readable. The structure needs three changes.
1. Add a Summary (top of page): 3 to 4 sentences. Name your level, domain, top 2
accomplishments with metrics, and what you are seeking. This is the first thing a recruiter
reads and it currently does not exist.
2. Trim bullets from 5 per role to 3 per role, prioritized by impact: Start with the biggest
outcome, then technical depth, then a leadership or collaboration angle. Shorter and punchy
beats long and comprehensive.
3. Move the Skills section to the bottom: Right now it appears above your experience.
Recruiters want to see impact first. Skills are context, not the headline.
4. Quantify every bullet: Your Zomato and Uber bullets are mostly good but several still read
as activities ('Spearheaded development of...') rather than outcomes. Every bullet should end
or begin with a metric. Use your impact document to pull those numbers.
5. Do not use one resume for all applications: Build a master resume from your impact
document, then swap in 3 to 4 bullets per role that directly address the specific job
description. The prompt Joshua mentioned will help you do this in Claude in about 10
minutes per application.

