OUTREACH SYSTEM: HOW TO GET CONVERSATIONS, NOT REJECTIONS
The methodology described in the session is worth spelling out fully because it is the single
highest-leverage change you can make.
Step 1: Build a target list of 5 companies at a time
Filter by: companies that will exist in 3 to 5 years, companies whose technical problems overlap
with what you have built, companies that have hired people at your level from India before. Put
these into a Google Sheet with columns for company, team or product area, key contacts
(recruiter, EM, SD3 peer), outreach status, and follow-up date.
Step 2: Find 3 contacts per company: one peer, one EM, one recruiter
Use LinkedIn to search within the company, filtered by Bengaluru or your target market. Look at
who is active, who posts, and who has Indian-origin names if you want easier rapport-building.
For European companies especially, find Indians who have made the move and ask them
directly about sponsorship processes.
Step 3: Send the curiosity message, not the cover letter
Your message should be three sentences or fewer. The structure is: name the specific problem
you believe their team is solving, name one specific thing you built that is relevant to it with a
metric, ask for 15 minutes. No resume. No background summary. No 'I hope this finds you well.'
Joshua gave you the exact script in the session. Here it is written out:
Hi [Name], I saw [Company] is scaling [Data Platform / real-time infrastructure / AI tooling]
and I think the core challenge is [problem in one sentence].
At Uber, I built a multi-agent data discovery system now used by 1,000+ engineers, and at
Zomato I rewrote a WebSocket service handling 500k+ clients with a 60% cost reduction.
Would you have 15 minutes to talk about what your team is working on?
---
Follow up 3 days later if no response: 'Not sure if you saw my last message, but here is
another example that might be relevant to what you are building: [different project, one
sentence, one metric]. Still happy to chat for 15 minutes if timing works.'

Step 4: Bring your impact document into the call, not your resume
When you get the call, the goal is to position yourself as someone who is thinking about their
problem, not someone who is looking for their next job. Ask questions about the team's current
technical challenges first. Then connect what you have built to what they need. The resume
comes later, when they ask for it, at which point you also tailor it to that specific conversation.
Step 5: Follow up and close loops
Most people give up after one message. Most recruiters and EMs remember the person who
followed up professionally. Send a second message 3 days after the first. If you had a call, send

